From bc596e5bbee99afcf2ebf2c366c870b181d95cb7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 13:10:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Count item 3-1's cost side at the consumers instead of at the
 read

The read expression cannot carry a counter -- it is also the assignment
target -- but a consumer's operand can, because it is a value and
nothing else. VisitConsumedBy is already the single choke point for the
five consumer categories 0105 plumbed, so one hook covers an
assignment's right-hand side into an element, a property or a local, a
call argument, and a return value.

Non-perturbation checked before anything was read from it: the
roots-consumed-by-a-refused-local count reproduces at 18 657 518
against 18 657 828, 1.000 on every suite, and the three failing suites
fail identically with the counter off.

It is a LOWER BOUND on the cost and is quoted as one -- every JSValue
consumer outside those five is missing. That direction is the useful
one: a bound above the saving refutes the representation, a bound below
it does not confirm it.

Against 16 426 721 boxed writes the instrumented consumers see
5 768 745 boxing reads, 0.35. Three causes are already refuted at the
bound: CallResult 9.41, NeverOffered 3.72, PropertyRead 1.83 -- together
1.4 M writes. The two causes carrying 14.2 M of the 16.4 M come in at
ElementRead 0.04 and DroppedCandidate 0.03, so the un-instrumented
consumers would have to supply twenty-five times every read counted to
reach break-even.

Per suite the split is the same shape: Crypto 0.02, Gameboy 0.04, PdfJS
0.00, NavierStokes 0.00 -- its refused locals are read only inside trees
-- Box2D 0.23, against Typescript 11.18 and EarleyBoyer 35.35.

This is the first affirmative evidence phase 3 has produced for widening
the numeric tier, and item 3-8a is why it is still a bound rather than a
verdict.

2 further cases, one being that a read inside a tree is charged to the
free side and not to the consumer. 8 171 tests across eight projects, no
failures.
